Q: How to convert a SVG into JSON which can be saved in backend and then again call the JSON and convert it to SVG A: <p>I think for this use case it makes sense to use a <strong>binary field</strong> in the database to save space when storing large strings that are meant for machine interpretation. You can convert the SVG to binary in your code, so the database doesn’t have to handle the conversion and the load stays on the backend.</p>
<p>For example, in JavaScript:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const svg = '&lt;svg width=&quot;300&quot; height=&quot;200&quot;&gt;&lt;polygon points=&quot;100,10 40,198 190,78 10,78 160,198&quot; style=&quot;fill:lime;stroke:purple;stroke-width:5;fill-rule:evenodd;&quot;/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;';

const buffer = Buffer.from(svg, 'utf8');
// Save `buffer` directly into a BLOB/VARBINARY column in your database
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// Assuming `bufferFromDb` is the BLOB/VARBINARY you fetched from the database
const svgString = bufferFromDb.toString('utf8');

// Now you can inject svgString into the DOM
document.getElementById('playground').innerHTML = svgString;
</code></pre>
<p>For the database column, you can use the following types depending on your database:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>MySQL:</strong> <code>BLOB</code>, <code>MEDIUMBLOB</code>, or <code>LONGBLOB</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>PostgreSQL:</strong> <code>BYTEA</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>SQL Server:</strong> <code>VARBINARY(MAX)</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>SQLite:</strong> <code>BLOB</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>This keeps your database queries lighter and reduces storage overhead, while still allowing you to reconstruct the SVG string when needed for the DOM.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Extra:</strong> You can also generate a <strong>unique ID for each SVG</strong> by hashing it (using a UUID generated from the SVG content). This way, you can use it for comparison or search, although every time the SVG changes, the UUID changes too. For example:</p>
<pre><code>import { createHash } from 'crypto';

function svgToUuid(svg: string) {
  return createHash('sha256').update(svg).digest('hex');
}

const svgUuid = svgToUuid(svg);
// Store `svgUuid` in the database and use it to check if an SVG from the frontend already exists
</code></pre>
<p>This is useful to verify if a given SVG is already stored in the database without saving duplicates.</p>
